# Opening wall visual lock — early S001 guide

Evidence: original film at 00:02, 00:07, 00:12 and 00:17; selected full-resolution reference studies and a four-frame contact sheet in `design-opening-v1`. The director determines title words and editorial time; DP paints a new wall, editor handles actual title overlays unless assigned otherwise. The reference frames are **never** picture assets.

- A dark olive/rust sheet-metal patchwork fills frame. Individual plates are modest, uneven, differently stained, sometimes visibly bent/curled/corner-crumpled, with partial overlaps and horizontal/vertical seams that never form a clean identical brick tiling. Burnished raised edges and cloudy illumination give height and age.
- Peripheral chunky analog machine fragments and small circular mechanisms edge in from left/right and lower corners. A wire, claw, spring or truncated pipe at an edge is a recurring motif; three giant tidy concentric gears dominating the wall is not the source look.
- Warm nicotine-yellow pools over olive/brown with edges falling nearly black. Keep a center/upper-middle strip quiet enough for readable original/new opening title overlays; background changes only subtly as a slow camera move. The first title is visible in the source at 00:02–00:07 and the main title around 00:12–00:17; cut boundaries await director's shot list.
- DP key proposal at 62d5ce4 is a useful color/layout prototype, **not** an approved final S001 design: repeated tidy plates, crisp heavy parallel seams and oversized mirrored gears need asymmetry, worn surface and subordinated mechanical devices. Exact film frame must never be copied, traced or filtered into the remake.

**Title lettering lock.** In the original at 00:02–00:17, thin tall uneven serif/gothic-like pale bone letters seem embossed or painted onto the plate surface with a subtle dark offset/shadow, unlike crisp modern sans serif floating over the image. The remake's title must be readable for at least eight seconds per director's SC01 notes; lettering should stay integrated with the moving wall (texture/shadow/perspective) and remain legible under the olive/yellow pool. Exact wording, credit attribution and on/off times are direction/edit decisions.
